---
import ChapterTable from '../../../components/ChapterTable.astro';
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Neighbors from '../../../components/Neighbors.astro';
import '../../../components/statistics/statistics.css';
---

<Layout title="Statistics" favicon="◒">
  <main class="stats-book">
    <a href="/reading/" class="text-sm">← back to reading</a>
    <p class="stats-kicker">A book you can experiment with</p>
    <h1>Statistics</h1>
    <p class="stats-deck">Make a guess. Try it. See what changes your mind.</p>
    <p>A few coin flips can surprise you. A thousand rounds begin to reveal a pattern. Statistics gives us ways to describe those patterns and decide what they tell us.</p>

    <h2>Start here: statistics through experiments</h2>
    <p>These short chapters start with something you can try. We’ll give each idea a name as we go, and arrive at definitions, formulas, and theorems once they have something familiar to say. You don’t need to know statistics or write code.</p>
    <a class="stats-start" href="/reading/statistics/chance/">
      <strong>01 · How likely is it? →</strong>
      <span>Spin a wheel, follow a streak, and give chance a name.</span>
    </a>
    <a class="stats-start" href="/reading/statistics/repeated-experiments/">
      <strong>02 · What happens when we repeat? →</strong>
      <span>Flip a handful of coins. Repeat. Watch a distribution take shape.</span>
    </a>

<a class="stats-start" href="/reading/statistics/describing-data/"><strong>03 · What does an average hide? →</strong><span>Move salaries, add an outlier, and compare two kinds of average.</span></a>
<a class="stats-start" href="/reading/statistics/samples/"><strong>04 · What can a handful tell us? →</strong><span>Sample a hidden town and compare your estimate with its target.</span></a>
<a class="stats-start" href="/reading/statistics/sampling-fairly/"><strong>05 · Can more data mislead us? →</strong><span>Change who gets sampled, then change how treatments are assigned.</span></a>
<a class="stats-start" href="/reading/statistics/estimates-vary/"><strong>06 · Why do estimates move? →</strong><span>Build a distribution of averages and meet the Central Limit Theorem.</span></a>
<a class="stats-start" href="/reading/statistics/intervals/"><strong>07 · How much uncertainty remains? →</strong><span>Make intervals, reveal the truth, and see what confidence means.</span></a>
<a class="stats-start" href="/reading/statistics/testing-a-claim/"><strong>08 · Could chance explain this? →</strong><span>Compare suspicious results with a fair-coin model.</span></a>

    <h2>Go deeper: the OpenIntro reading guide</h2>
    <p>Work through the ideas in more detail, with diagrams and runnable examples. Each chapter can also serve as a reference when you have a particular question.</p>
    <ChapterTable chapters={[
      { href: '/reading/statistics/openintro-01/', title: 'Introduction to Data', desc: 'What are we observing, and how did we choose it?' },
      { href: '/reading/statistics/openintro-02/', title: 'Summarizing Data', desc: 'What can an average tell us? What does it leave out?' },
      { href: '/reading/statistics/openintro-03/', title: 'Probability', desc: 'How do chances combine or change with new information?' },
      { href: '/reading/statistics/openintro-04/', title: 'Distributions', desc: 'The shapes that repeated experiments produce' },
      { href: '/reading/statistics/openintro-05/', title: 'Foundations for Inference', desc: 'What can a sample tell us about a larger population?' },
      { href: '/reading/statistics/openintro-06/', title: 'Inference for Proportions', desc: 'Comparing counts, percentages, and categories' },
      { href: '/reading/statistics/openintro-07/', title: 'Inference for Means', desc: 'Comparing averages while accounting for uncertainty' },
      { href: '/reading/statistics/openintro-08/', title: 'Simple Linear Regression', desc: 'Fitting a line and understanding what it misses' },
      { href: '/reading/statistics/openintro-09/', title: 'Multiple and Logistic Regression', desc: 'Several predictors, and outcomes that are yes or no' },
    ]} />
    <p class="stats-credit">The reading guide is based on <a href="https://www.openintro.org/book/os/">OpenIntro Statistics</a> by David Diez, Mine Çetinkaya-Rundel, and Christopher Barr, and shared under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>. The introductory chapters credit their own sources.</p>
    <p class="stats-small">For another way into the ideas: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/user/joshstarmer">StatQuest with Josh Starmer</a>.</p>
    <Neighbors>
      <ul class="neighbors">
        <li><a href="/reading/probability/">🎰 Probability</a> — the mathematics behind chance</li>
        <li><a href="/reading/scientific-method/">🔬 Scientific Method</a> — evidence, significance, and replication</li>
        <li><a href="/reading/machine-learning/">🤖 Machine Learning</a> — learning patterns from data</li>
        <li><a href="/reading/information-theory/">📡 Information Theory</a> — measuring uncertainty and information</li>
      </ul>
    </Neighbors>
  </main>
</Layout>
